editorial:
## Method and reasoning
Initialize dp[0]=1. For ordered sequences loop over sums first and add all possible choices of the last coin. For unordered combinations loop over denominations first, then increasing sums. This counts a multiset once in denomination order and permits repeating the current coin.

## Walk through the sample
For target 4 with coins 1 and 2, there are three multisets: four 1s, two 1s and a 2, or two 2s. The middle multiset has three orders; the other two have one each. Hence 5 ordered and 3 unordered.

## Complexity
Time O(nT), space O(T).

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int n, t;
    cin >> n >> t;
    vector<int> c(n), ordered(t + 1), unordered(t + 1);
    for (int &x : c)
        cin >> x;
    constexpr int MOD = 1000000007;
    auto add = [](int &x, int y) {
        x += y;
        if (x >= MOD)
            x -= MOD;
    };
    ordered[0] = unordered[0] = 1;
    for (int s = 1; s <= t; ++s)
        for (int x : c)
            if (x <= s)
                add(ordered[s], ordered[s - x]);
    for (int x : c)
        for (int s = x; s <= t; ++s)
            add(unordered[s], unordered[s - x]);
    cout << ordered[t] << ' ' << unordered[t] << '\n';
}
~~~
